    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/120315": {
      "title": "Vice Chair for Research",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Vice Chair for Research - Neurology and Rehabilitation Medicine Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Vice Chair for Research - Neurology and Rehabilitation Medicine Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. To be considered, applicants should complete an online faculty application and upload a letter of interest, a CV, and the contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin June 20, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be considered. Recruitment Plan I. RECRUITMENT PLAN Posting Number: F002511 Academic Title: Vice Chair for Research - Neurology and Rehabilitation Medicine Rank: Professor, Associate Professor Administrative Title: Vice Chair for Research Discipline Clinical Medicine-MD Specialty: Contract Type: Non-Tenure Track, Renewable Appointment (initial appt period) - 9/1/25-6/30/26 Required Licenses/Certifications and other Specific Requirements: Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search, Prior Employment Verification, This position requires a licensure, certification, or special requirements Proposed Start Date: 09/01/2025 Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Position Description Summary: The George Washington Medical Faculty Associates’ Department of Neurology and Rehabilitation medicine is seeking a physician or non-physician scientist for the position of Vice Chair of Research. The sought Vice Chair for Research is a visionary and dynamic leader. This senior leadership role is critical to advancing our mission to become a nationally recognized center of excellence in neurological research. The Vice Chair for Research will shape the department’s research trajectory, mentor the next generation of clinician-scientists, and foster collaborative, interdisciplinary initiatives.The appointment, expected to begin as early as Fall 2025, will be at the rank of associate or full professor, and a competitive start-up package will be provided to support the successful candidate’s research program. Salary will be commensurate with experience. Specific Duties and Responsibilities: The Vice Chair for Research will play a pivotal role in positioning the Department of Neurology as a leader in innovative research, ultimately transforming care for patients with neurological disorders, and will share our ambition and are ready to lead with creativity, passion, and vision. The Vice Chair of Research’s responsibilities will include: conducting independent research aligned with the department’s academic vision, securing extramural funding from federal, industry, and philanthropic sources, establishing new research initiatives with a focus on translational and clinical science, providing mentorship and faculty development through structured programs, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration with basic science and clinical departments, overseeing research operations including resource management and regulatory compliance, advocating for the department’s research mission, and engaging with the community and philanthropic partners to support research initiatives. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must be an MD, PhD, or MD/PhD with a distinguished research record in a relevant field of neurology and demonstrated success in securing extramural funding and leading research teams. Applicants must have a strong track record of mentorship and faculty development and display excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to inspire and unite diverse stakeholders. Applicants must also commit to fostering an inclusive and equitable research environment. Advertised Salary: $175,000 to $350,000. Other Benefits: The MFA also provides generous health and other benefits, details of which can be found at https://careers.gwdocs.com/benefits . Other Information: Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, applicants should complete an online faculty application and upload a letter of interest, a CV, and the contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin June 20, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be considered. Department Link: https://smhs.gwu.edu/neurology/ Posting Close Date: Open Until Filled: Yes Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Quick Link https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/119494 Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have an MD, PhD, or MD/PhD? Yes No * Do you have a distinguished research record in a relevant field of neurology? Yes No * Can you demonstrate success in securing extramural funding and leading research teams? Yes No * Do you have a strong track record of mentorship and faculty development? Yes No * Can you display excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to inspire and unite diverse stakeholders? Yes No * Do you display excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to inspire and unite diverse stakeholders? Yes No * Will you commit to fostering an inclusive and equitable research environment? Yes No Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit Talent@GWLogin Privacy Notice Connect with us To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128839": {
      "title": "Global Initiatives Director, Global Food Institute",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Global Initiatives Director, Global Food Institute Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Global Initiatives Director, Global Food Institute Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status. This position is grant-funded and is contingent upon funding. Please submit three writing samples from the last five years as a single PDF. Samples should demonstrate experience in research translation and policy communication, including a range of public-facing products such as policy reports, white papers, op-eds, blogs, and proposals. Posting Details I. DEPARTMENT INFORMATION Job Description Summary: Founded by The George Washington University (GW) and renowned chef, humanitarian and long-time GW instructor José Andrés, the Global Food Institute at GW ( GFI ) seeks to advance global food policy and address the world’s most urgent problems by leveraging the power of food. GFI works across four core ambitions: Education: developing and empowering the next generation of food leaders Research: leading practice-informed, solutions-oriented research developed in direct collaboration with practitioners and community partners Exchange: building platforms to generate, elevate, and share insights Community & Partnerships: cultivating partnerships to drive shared solutions GW’s unique strengths at the intersection of education, business, technology, healthcare, and international affairs – combined with its location at the center of U.S. policymaking – create the ideal environment for private- and public-sector leaders to develop lasting solutions to the world’s most pressing challenges. GFI is seeking a Global Initiatives Director to develop and oversee GFI’s global portfolio. Reporting to the Executive Director, the Global Initiatives Director, GFI is recognized as a subject matter expert on international and/or global food policy issues, with special expertise in the humanitarian space, and either leads or directly contributes to GFI research, policy, and engagement work in the global arena. Working collaboratively with the team and other GW partners, they will develop and execute a global program designed to advance the food needs of marginalized and disadvantaged people and to advance equity and justice in the global food system with a major focus on conceptualizing, writing and managing driving policy briefs and translational research. Writing for a public audience is a critical component of this job. The role is entrepreneurial in nature: the Global Initiatives Director will inherit a foundation of relationships, projects, and institutional presence, and is expected to shape the portfolio’s direction, particularly published products, going forward. As GFI grows, the Director may hire and supervise a team to support this work contingent on securing funding for additional staff positions. The incumbent will partner closely with GFI’s other Directors – Curriculum, Policy, and Research – to build out a shared portfolio of ideas, communications, and engagement strategies. Together, this work will shape debates, guide policy, and inform the implementation of programs and practices. Responsibilities Include: Under the direction of the Executive Director, develop and advance a research and programmatic agenda in collaboration with team members, GW faculty and institutes, and other policy experts that advances the GFI’s mission to ensure a global food system that supports everyone — especially historically marginalized groups. Develop learning and knowledge into opinions, organizational positions and best practices which can be used to create policies that drive the food system forward in the optimal direction. This includes translating research and evidence for non-academic audiences through published policy briefs, op-eds, commentaries, and other public-facing products on a regular basis. Participate in GFI’s leadership team, helping to set the strategic vision and values for the organization as well as promoting a culture and environment that reflects those values. Advancement to ensure this work translates into continued financial support. Design and lead GFI events, such as conferences, convenings, webinars – including building and stewarding external research and policy communities of practice – designed to engage and inform the GW, stakeholder, policy maker and media communities. Develop and foster strategic relationships across government, multilateral institutions, universities, and the private sector, recruiting diverse partners who can expand GFI’s reach, inform policy, and support shared research efforts. Support the GFI and broader GW student community, including by providing support to the Food Leadership Minor, working on shared programming with GW Departments and Schools, connecting students to research and internships, and other student engagement activities. Lead and contribute to grant proposals and funding applications, and manage relationships with co-principal investigators. This is an in-person position at GW’s Foggy Bottom campus in Washington, D.C., with flexibility for remote-work opportunities at the Executive Director’s discretion. Performs other related duties as assigned. The omission of specific duties does not preclude the supervisor from assigning duties that are logically related to the position. Minimum Qualifications: Qualified candidates will hold a BA/BS in a related discipline plus 7 years of relevant professional experience. Degree must be conferred by the start date of the position. Additional Required Licenses/Certifications/Posting Specific Minimum Qualifications: Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated writing and analytical skills with particular experience in research translation and policy communication, including a range of public facing products, including: policy reports, white papers, op eds, blogs and proposals. Experience in one or more aspects of the global food system, with a priority in humanitarian aid and emergency response, food security policy, or international development – with a record of publications and presentations in the applicant’s field of expertise. A deep commitment to GW’s values; honesty, with the highest ethical standards. A naturally collaborative mindset that readily engages with others, encourages others to share the spotlight and celebrates and supports the success of colleagues and partners. Demonstrated experience managing research projects and teams – including across teams – bringing projects to fruition, successfully managing projects and budgets. Experience establishing networks and partnerships and ability to demonstrate a strong portfolio of contacts in the non-profit, private, and government sectors is desired. Experience securing and providing appropriate stewardship of philanthropic gifts and research or project grants from foundations and the government is desired. Experience writing proposals, including multi-institution collaborative proposals, is strongly preferred. Entrepreneurial and collaborative spirit; enthusiastic about building and launching a new enterprise. Open and direct communicator. Willingness to travel for meetings and engagement with key partners. Experience with higher education institutions is a plus. Fluency in English. Good communications skills in an additional language is a plus. Hiring Range $142,545.11 - $199,490.92 GW Staff Approach to Pay How is pay for new employees determined at GW? Healthcare Benefits GW offers a comprehensive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, life & disability insurance, time off & leave, retirement savings, tuition, well-being and various voluntary benefits. For program details and eligibility, please visit https://hr.gwu.edu/benefits-programs. II. POSITION INFORMATION Campus Location: Foggy Bottom, Washington, D.C. College/School/Department: Academic Affairs Family Research and Labs Sub-Family Centers/Institutes Stream Individual Contributor Level Level 4 Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Hours Per Week: 40 Work Schedule: Monday - Friday: 8:00AM - 5:00PM Will this job require the employee to work on site? Yes Employee Onsite Status On-campus (in person) Telework: No Required Background Check Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search Special Instructions to Applicants: Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status. This position is grant-funded and is contingent upon funding. Please submit three writing samples from the last five years as a single PDF . Samples should demonstrate experience in research translation and policy communication, including a range of public-facing products such as policy reports, white papers, op-eds, blogs, and proposals. Internal Applicants Only? No Posting Number: R002460 Job Open Date: 09/17/2026 Job Close Date: 09/25/2026 Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you currently employed with The George Washington University? No Yes * For current GW employees, have you completed your Introductory Employment Period (IEP)?  (As a reminder, employees in their IEP are not eligible to apply for other internal university staff and research positions until the IEP is complete.) Yes, IEP complete No, still in IEP N/a - not a current GW employee * What is your salary range expectation? (Open Ended Question) * Describe your experience in one or more aspects of\r\nthe global food system, including humanitarian aid and emergency response, food security policy, or international development. (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Other Documents Refer to Special Instructions to Applicants Optional Documents Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit Talent@GWLogin Privacy Notice Connect with us To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128712": {
      "title": "George Washington Employment Site",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. When applying for the Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering position please highlight the experiences you have that address the job responsibilities and demonstrate your ability to thrive in this position and within the broader GWLAI and university communities. To be considered, please visit https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128364 and upload a CV and a cover letter that includes an assessment of skills and experiences related to minimum and preferred qualifications. Only complete applications will be considered. Review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Employment offers are contingent upon the satisfactory outcome of a reference check and standard background screening. Recruitment Plan I. RECRUITMENT PLAN Posting Number: F002654 Academic Title: Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering Rank: Librarian I, Librarian II, Librarian III, Librarian IV Administrative Title: Discipline Libraries Specialty: Contract Type: Renewable Appointment (initial appt period) - Renewable period depending on rank at date of hire. Required Licenses/Certifications and other Specific Requirements: Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search Proposed Start Date: Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Position Description Summary: The George Washington University Libraries and Academic Innovation ( GWLAI ) enriches the academic experience of scholars and students and sparks innovation at GW. We enable research and teaching through access to the latest scholarship and rich archival resources, tailored support for researchers at all levels, guidance for faculty who want to explore new teaching methods and technologies in the classroom, and robust support for online education. Through innovative partnerships, dynamic workshops, and team-based approaches to project management and problem solving, GWLAI fosters collaboration and provides leadership across the university to support the shared mission of generating, making accessible, and preserving new knowledge. Having joined the Association of American Universities ( AAU ) in 2023, and guided by a new Strategic Framework , GW is at an exciting moment in its history and trajectory, and GWLAI is a key partner in supporting the university’s research and scholarly advancements and promoting discoveries and innovations made by the research community. GWLAI seeks a collaborative and service-oriented Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering to join the Research and User Services ( RUS ) department. RUS is a highly collaborative unit where librarians work together and share responsibility for meeting the full range of student, faculty, and researcher needs across disciplines. This position plays a vital role in supporting interdisciplinary teaching, research, and publishing, with an emphasis on supporting the natural sciences, engineering, and data-intensive research fields. As an active member of the RUS team, the incumbent also provides research support, instruction, and collection development services to all areas of the university, including the First Year Writing program. GWLAI staff and librarians are champions of open access and support open scholarly communication and publishing practices, including the adoption and creation of open educational resources ( OER ). Librarians contribute to the scholarly community through research, professional engagement, and service. Specific Duties and Responsibilities: Support the research and instructional needs of faculty, students, and researchers in the sciences, engineering, and related interdisciplinary fields. Conduct library instruction for undergraduate and graduate level courses in partnership with faculty, including partnering with GW’s first-year University Writing Program. Provide research consultations and services (chat, email) across disciplines. Participate in collaborative collection development and relationship-building with departments and research units to understand and meet their evolving needs. Provide expert guidance on scientific research methods and navigating scientific literature, reproducible research workflows, datasets, and data analysis tools. Contribute to workshops, outreach, and programming that advance data literacy, computational fluency, and research transparency. Contribute to strategic initiatives or teams within GWLAI , including efforts related to scholarly communication, open access publishing, open education, research impact and visibility, and/or data literacy and computational fluency. Minimum Qualifications: ALA -accredited Master’s degree in library or information science OR other advanced degree with relevant experience by date of appointment. Demonstrated collaboration skills and a commitment to supporting an inclusive and equitable research community. Strong initiative and a results-driven approach in previous roles. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Awareness of current scholarly communication and research trends, issues, and challenges facing researchers in an academic environment. Experience or demonstrated potential to support scientific research, instruction, and publishing practices within science and engineering fields. Advertised Salary: Librarian 1: $58,566 - $65,000; Librarian 2: $61,156 - $78,000; Librarian 3: $65,156 - $81,000; Librarian 4: $71,656 - $130,000 Other Benefits: Salary and appointment at the rank of Librarian I/II/ III /IV are dependent upon qualifications. Salary is negotiable, contingent upon rank, skills and qualifications and is competitive for DC-area academic libraries. Comprehensive benefits package includes 22 days/year paid annual leave; 12 days/year paid sick leave; paid winter break and 8 other holidays; medical, dental, and vision; 401(A) retirement plan, 4% base and GW will match 150% of the first 4% of your 403(B) contributions, up to a maximum of 6% of your eligible compensation; tuition assistance; ongoing support for professional development; and paid parental leave. Librarians are eligible to apply for research leave after a defined period of service. For benefit details, please visit GW Benefits . This position is classified as a Council of Librarians position and is eligible for a hybrid on-campus/remote work schedule. This position is a continual reappointment position with reappointment periods of 3-5 years depending on rank. Proven commitment to the profession is expected and can be exhibited through service, publication, and/or presentations Other Information: PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Work experience, degree, or coursework in sciences, computer sciences, or other engineering related fields. Experience with data analysis, GIS , coding, visualization, or computational research tools. Familiarity with research data management, open science practices, and reproducible research methods. Experience supporting systematic review projects or teaching about systematic review methodology. We recognize that strong candidates may not meet every qualification. If you are passionate about this role, we encourage you to apply and highlight how your experiences align with the position. This position reports to a Director of Research Services and is eligible for partial telework. The University and GWLAI have a strong commitment to recruiting librarians of the highest caliber who have thrived in different environments and would enrich the University with different experiences and perspectives. We offer a work environment that values and supports collaboration, teamwork, and professional growth and development. A virtual information session for interested candidates will be offered in the coming weeks. Please visit https://go.gwu.edu/librarianjobs for details. Special Instructions to Applicants: When applying for the Research Services Librarian for Science and Engineering position please highlight the experiences you have that address the job responsibilities and demonstrate your ability to thrive in this position and within the broader GWLAI and university communities. To be considered, please visit https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128364 and upload a CV and a cover letter that includes an assessment of skills and experiences related to minimum and preferred qualifications. Only complete applications will be considered. Review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Employment offers are contingent upon the satisfactory outcome of a reference check and standard background screening. Department Link: http://library.gwu.edu/ Posting Close Date: Open Until Filled: Yes Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Quick Link https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128364 Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Optional Documents Resume Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Will you have an ALA-accredited Master's degree in library or information science or other advanced degree with relevant experience by date of appointment? Yes No * Do you have demonstrated collaboration skills and a commitment to supporting an inclusive and equitable research community? Yes No * Do you have a strong initiative and a results-driven approach in previous roles? Yes No * Can you demonstrate excellent oral and written communication skills? Yes No * Do you have awareness of current scholarly communication and research trends, issues, and\nchallenges facing researchers in an academic environment? Yes No * Do you have experience or demonstrated potential to support scientific research, instruction, and\npublishing practices within science and engineering fields? Yes No Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit Talent@GWLogin Privacy Notice Connect with us To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/124847": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor, DC Cohort Study",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Assistant Research Professor, DC Cohort Study Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Research Professor, DC Cohort Study Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should complete the online faculty application and submit the following documents: 1) a curriculum vitae; 2) a statement of research interests and accomplishments; and 3) a statement of clinical, teaching and/or mentoring experience. Review of applications will begin on 04/22/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be considered. Recruitment Plan I. RECRUITMENT PLAN Posting Number: F002578 Academic Title: Assistant Research Professor, DC Cohort Study Rank: Assistant Research Professor Administrative Title: Discipline Public Health Specialty: Contract Type: Research Required Licenses/Certifications and other Specific Requirements: Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search, Prior Employment Verification, This position requires a licensure, certification, or special requirements Proposed Start Date: 04/01/2026 Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Position Description Summary: The Department of Epidemiology of the GW Milken Institute School of Public Health ( GWSPH ) is recruiting for a dynamic, full-time research faculty member at the rank of Assistant Research Professor to serve as the Senior Clinical Research Coordinator for a large NIH -funded HIV / AIDS study, the DC Cohort, at 95% effort. The remaining 5% effort will be dedicated to educational and/or service responsibilities, to be determined based on the training and interests of the successful applicant and the needs of the Department and School. The university and departments have a strong commitment to recruiting faculty of the highest caliber who have thrived in different environments and would enrich the university with a wide variety of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives. Specific Duties and Responsibilities: The DC Cohort is one of the largest urban HIV cohort studies in the US and is NIH -funded as a longitudinal observational study of people living with HIV and receiving care at 14 clinical sites in Washington, DC. The study captures clinical information on people living with HIV in care and has been enrolling since 2011 in partnership with numerous clinical providers in Washington, DC, the DC Department of Health (DC DOH ), and the National Institutes of Health ( NIH ). The study was recently expanded at six sites to include persons at higher risk for HIV acquisition to create a Prevention Cohort. The responsibilities of the DC Cohort Research Assistant Professor will include overseeing study staff, working with the study Principal Investigator, Project Director, and data management partners at the GWU Biostatistics Center ( BSC ) to direct and manage the day-to-day study operations, assisting and leading design and analysis of manuscripts for the peer-reviewed literature using the DC Cohort database, and collaborating with partners at the NIH and DC DOH , the BSC , the 14 site clinical investigators and other stakeholders to improve patient care and ensure the continued success and growth of this study. Minimum Qualifications: The applicant must have an advanced clinical degree (MD, DO, or equivalent, DNP , NP, PA), HIV clinical experience and a Masters degree in Public Health or related field. The applicant must have at least 3-5 years of study research coordination experience in HIV / AIDS epidemiology and/or other large cohort or clinical trials studies in the field or in a related area, a track record of peer-reviewed publications commensurate with their time in practice, knowledge of HIV -related clinical issues and related outcomes, and strong written and oral communication skills. Advertised Salary: 120,000-140,000 Other Benefits: This position is eligible for full-time health benefits. For more information please visit: https://hr.gwu.edu/benefits Other Information: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should complete the online faculty application and submit the following documents: 1) a curriculum vitae; 2) a statement of research interests and accomplishments; and 3) a statement of clinical, teaching and/or mentoring experience. Review of applications will begin on 04/22/2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be considered. Department Link: https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/123789 Posting Close Date: Open Until Filled: Yes Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Quick Link https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/123789 Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Statement of Teaching Interest Statement of Research Interest Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have an advanced clinical degree (MD, DO, or equivalent, DNP, NP, PA)? Yes No * Do you have HIV clinical experience? Yes No * Do you have a Masters degree in Public Health or a related field? Yes No * Do you have at least 3-5 years of research study coordination experience in HIV/AIDS epidemiology and/or other large cohort or clinical trials studies in the field or in a related area? Yes No * Do you have a track record of peer-reviewed publications commensurate with your time in practice? Yes No * Do you have knowledge of HIV-related clinical issues and related outcomes? Yes No * Do you have strong written and oral communication skills? Yes No Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit Talent@GWLogin Privacy Notice Connect with us To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://www.gwu.jobs/postings/128844": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "George Washington Employment Site | Research Assistant Skip to Main Content SEARCH JOBS Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Search Job Libraries Log In /Create Account Help Research Assistant Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status Posting Details I. DEPARTMENT INFORMATION Job Description Summary: The Department of Epidemiology is looking to hire a full-time Research Assistant for a research study funded by the National Institutes of Health. The study will be conducted in the GWU Hospital Emergency Department. The primary responsibilities of the job will be: (1) acting as a pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) navigator and resource to the other research assistants (RAs) who work in the emergency department (ED); (2) working in the ED screening and identifying eligible participants, explaining study procedures and obtaining consent, conducting HIV prevention and PrEP medication counseling, providing patients with a PrEP Decision Support Tool and reviewing results with patients, helping to coordinate receipt of PrEP medication including making appointments for patients with community PrEP providers, and providing referrals to social services; and (3) conducting follow-up interviews with participants. It is expected that the candidate selected for this job will develop in-depth knowledge about PrEP and will be comfortable talking to patients, other research staff, and clinical staff about PrEP. Essential Duties and Responsibilities include the following: Interact with patients and clinical providers to screen, recruit, and consent participants, conduct surveys, collect data, and enter data into a computer. Work with the study coordinator to ensure that the baseline and telephone follow-up interviews are conducted in a timely manner and as successfully as possible. Conduct telephone follow-up with participants Provide daily research support for the assigned research project and related activities, including informed consent, enrollment, data collection (i.e., conducting interviews), data entry, management, and review. Interact with the clinical staff and ensure that study procedures do not disrupt clinical operations where patients are being treated Attend mandatory study team meetings as scheduled and lead the review of enrollment and follow-up results. Performs other related duties as assigned. The omission of specific duties does not preclude the supervisor from assigning duties that are logically related to the position. Minimum Qualifications: Qualified candidates will hold a BA/BS in a related discipline. Degree must be conferred by the start date of the position. Additional Required Licenses/Certifications/Posting Specific Minimum Qualifications: Employees are required to complete CITI and HIPAA certification if hired. Employees will also be required to complete a PrEP Navigator training program if hired. Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience working with HIV and other community-based organizations and populations at risk for HIV is preferred. Strong interest in research and enthusiasm for interacting with people. Strong Communication & Teamwork: Ability to communicate effectively and collaborate seamlessly with project staff, research team members, and emergency department clinical staff. Independent & Detail-Oriented: Capable of managing tasks independently with high accuracy and attention to detail. Computer literacy with Microsoft Word is desired. Hiring Range $18.40 - $27.68 GW Staff Approach to Pay How is pay for new employees determined at GW? Healthcare Benefits GW offers a comprehensive benefit package that includes medical, dental, vision, life & disability insurance, time off & leave, retirement savings, tuition, well-being and various voluntary benefits. For program details and eligibility, please visit https://hr.gwu.edu/benefits-programs. II. POSITION INFORMATION Campus Location: Foggy Bottom, Washington, D.C. College/School/Department: Milken Institute School of Public Health (SPH) Family Research and Labs Sub-Family Field Research Stream Service and Support Level Level 2 Full-Time/Part-Time: Full-Time Hours Per Week: 40 Work Schedule: Monday - Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. Will this job require the employee to work on site? Yes Employee Onsite Status On-campus (in person) Telework: Required Background Check Criminal History Screening, Education/Degree/Certifications Verification, Social Security Number Trace, and Sex Offender Registry Search Special Instructions to Applicants: Employer will not sponsor for employment Visa status Internal Applicants Only? No Posting Number: R002454 Job Open Date: 09/17/2026 Job Close Date: Background Screening Successful Completion of a Background Screening will be required as a condition of hire. EEO Statement: The university is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer that does not unlawfully discriminate in any of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, or on any other basis prohibited by applicable law. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you currently employed with The George Washington University? No Yes * For current GW employees, have you completed your Introductory Employment Period (IEP)?  (As a reminder, employees in their IEP are not eligible to apply for other internal university staff and research positions until the IEP is complete.) Yes, IEP complete No, still in IEP N/a - not a current GW employee * What is your salary range expectation? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Human Resource Management & Development 2013 H Street, NW, 3rd Floor Washington, DC 20006 Phone: 202-994-8500 Fax: 202-994-9680 Email: askhrmd@gwu.edu Recognition_Toolkit